# Digitalladdex Project - Cursor Rules

## Project Overview
This is a frontend web project (HTML/CSS/JavaScript) that appears to be part of a WordPress plugin or theme component. The project uses vanilla JavaScript, custom CSS with CSS variables, and follows a component-based structure.

## Technology Stack
- **HTML5**: Semantic HTML structure
- **CSS3**: Custom properties (CSS variables), modern CSS features
- **Vanilla JavaScript**: No frameworks, pure ES6+ JavaScript
- **Fonts**: Poppins font family (multiple weights: 400, 500, 600, 700, 800)
- **Icons**: SVG icons (inline SVG)

## Code Style Guidelines

### HTML
- Use semantic HTML5 elements
- Include proper accessibility attributes (aria-label, alt text, etc.)
- Use consistent indentation (2 spaces)
- Include proper meta tags and viewport settings
- Use descriptive class names with the `dl-` prefix for custom components
- Always include proper alt text for images
- Use proper heading hierarchy (h1, h2, h3, etc.)

### CSS
- Use CSS custom properties (variables) defined in `:root`
- Follow BEM-like naming convention with `dl-` prefix
- Use consistent spacing and indentation (2 spaces)
- Group related properties together
- Use modern CSS features (flexbox, grid, custom properties)
- Maintain responsive design principles
- Use meaningful class names that describe purpose, not appearance
- Keep layout styles in `layout.css` and component styles in `style.css`

### JavaScript
- Use modern ES6+ syntax (arrow functions, const/let, template literals)
- Use `DOMContentLoaded` event listener for initialization
- Use descriptive variable and function names
- Add comments for complex logic
- Handle edge cases (null checks, element existence checks)
- Use event delegation where appropriate
- Keep functions focused and single-purpose
- Use `querySelector` and `querySelectorAll` for DOM manipulation

## File Structure
```
/
├── index.html
├── assets/
│   ├── css/
│   │   ├── layout.css (layout and grid systems)
│   │   └── style.css (component styles)
│   ├── js/
│   │   └── script.js (main JavaScript file)
│   ├── fonts/
│   │   └── poppins/ (font files)
│   └── images/ (SVG and image assets)
```

## Naming Conventions
- **CSS Classes**: Use `dl-` prefix for custom components (e.g., `dl-card`, `dl-menu`, `dl-logo`)
- **JavaScript Variables**: Use camelCase (e.g., `searchIcon`, `toggleMenuBtn`)
- **CSS Variables**: Use kebab-case with descriptive names (e.g., `--pc`, `--sc`, `--bg-1`)
- **IDs**: Use descriptive names (e.g., `counter1`, `scroll_to_top`)

## Best Practices

### Performance
- Use `fetchpriority` and `decoding` attributes on images
- Preload critical fonts
- Minimize inline styles
- Use efficient selectors
- Optimize SVG icons

### Accessibility
- Include proper ARIA labels
- Use semantic HTML elements
- Ensure keyboard navigation works
- Provide alt text for all images
- Use proper heading hierarchy
- Ensure sufficient color contrast

### Responsive Design
- Use mobile-first approach
- Test on multiple screen sizes
- Use relative units (rem, em, %) where appropriate
- Use CSS Grid and Flexbox for layouts
- Implement proper breakpoints

### Code Organization
- Group related functionality together
- Use comments to separate sections
- Keep functions small and focused
- Avoid deep nesting
- Use consistent formatting

## Common Patterns

### CSS Variables
The project uses CSS custom properties defined in `:root`:
- Color variables: `--pc`, `--sc`, `--black`, `--white`, `--bg-1`, etc.
- Typography: `--fw-1`, `--fw-2`, `--fs-1`, `--fs-2`, etc.
- Spacing/Radius: `--rad-1`, `--rad-2`, `--rad-0`

### JavaScript Patterns
- Event listeners wrapped in `DOMContentLoaded`
- Toggle functions for mobile menus
- Counter animations
- Scroll-based interactions
- Dropdown menu handling

## When Adding New Features
1. Follow existing naming conventions
2. Use existing CSS variables where possible
3. Maintain consistent code style
4. Add appropriate comments
5. Test for accessibility
6. Ensure responsive behavior
7. Check browser compatibility

## Code Quality
- Write clean, readable code
- Avoid magic numbers (use variables)
- Comment complex logic
- Keep functions under 50 lines when possible
- Use meaningful names
- Remove console.logs before production
- Handle errors gracefully

